  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Ubigi</strong> if you want an established telecom's network behind your eSIM and don't mind paying roughly double for it. Pick <strong>RedteaGO</strong> if the lowest realistic per-GB price is what matters most and you're fine checking a plan's fine print for calls and texts. They're not really fighting for the same buyer: one sells reassurance, the other sells a low number.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Established MVNO vs Lowest Price</h2>
  <p>
    Ubigi is a brand of Transatel, a French MVNO enabler running cellular connectivity since the early 2000s and now part of NTT Group. The same underlying network also powers the connected-car SIMs built into cars from BMW, Toyota, Jeep, Maserati, Fiat, Alfa Romeo, Mini, Jaguar, and Land Rover. RedteaGO doesn't make an equivalent pedigree claim anywhere on its own site; its pitch is simply the lowest entry price it can advertise, starting from $0.52 for a Europe-wide plan.
  </p>
  <p>
    That difference in positioning shows up directly in price. Ubigi runs about double RedteaGO's per-GB cost on comparable plans, which is the trade a buyer is actually making here: pay more for Ubigi's network pedigree and plan flexibility, or pay less and accept RedteaGO's more limited structure.
  </p>

