  <h2>Is Ubigi or RedteaGO the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Ubigi if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You visit the same country often enough that a recurring monthly plan beats buying a new eSIM each time</li>
        <li>You want a 1-day minimum validity rather than committing to a longer window upfront</li>
        <li>You'd rather buy from an established MVNO than a cheaper reseller, even at a higher price</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with RedteaGO if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You want the lowest realistic starting price and are happy checking the actual GB before buying</li>
        <li>You might need a working local number: RedteaGO offers one, Ubigi doesn't</li>
        <li>You're fine with a 15-day minimum plan validity rather than Ubigi's 1-day option</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Neither includes calls or texts by default, but RedteaGO has a path to them and Ubigi doesn't.</strong> RedteaGO labels specific plans "Calls/Texts Available" and sells a separate Call & Text eSIM package; if you need a working phone number, you have to check for that label first. Ubigi doesn't offer calls or SMS on any plan, labeled or otherwise, data only across the board.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The commitment terms run in opposite directions.</strong> Ubigi's one-off plans have just a 1-day minimum validity, but its recurring monthly plans lock you in for 3 months before you can cancel, and its annual plans can't be cancelled or refunded at all during the year. RedteaGO has no recurring option to worry about, but every top-up is non-refundable the moment it activates, and plans carry a 15-day minimum validity even for a short trip. Read the terms for whichever one you're actually buying, not just the headline price.
  </p>
  <p>
    Want unlimited data instead of a fixed bucket from either one? See <a href="/holafly/">Holafly</a>. Want RedteaGO's low pricing with an even cheaper per-GB rate on most countries? See <a href="/airhub/">Airhub</a>. For eSIM device compatibility, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Ubigi vs RedteaGO FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Ubigi or RedteaGO</h3>
      <p>RedteaGO, by roughly half on a per-GB basis. Ubigi's higher price buys an established MVNO's network and a recurring-plan option that RedteaGO doesn't offer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either one include calls or texts</h3>
      <p>Not by default on either. RedteaGO at least has a path to it: plans labeled "Calls/Texts Available" or its separate Call & Text eSIM package. Ubigi has no calls or SMS option on any plan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which has the shorter minimum commitment</h3>
      <p>Ubigi's one-off plans, with a 1-day minimum validity. RedteaGO plans carry a 15-day minimum validity even for a short trip. Ubigi's recurring plans flip that: a 3-month minimum before cancelling a monthly plan, and no cancellation at all on an annual one.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which one offers a real local phone number</h3>
      <p>RedteaGO does. Ubigi doesn't offer a local number on any plan.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

